What is File Handling?

File handling in Java means reading from and writing to files stored on your system —
for example, reading data from a .txt file or writing logs to a file.

All file-related classes are in the package:
👉 java.io

| Class                                  | Purpose                               |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `File`                                 | Represents file or directory path     |
| `FileReader`                           | Reads characters from file            |
| `BufferedReader`                       | Reads text efficiently (line by line) |
| `FileWriter`                           | Writes characters to file             |
| `BufferedWriter`                       | Writes text efficiently               |
| `FileInputStream` / `FileOutputStream` | Read/write binary data (images, etc.) |

FileReader vs BufferedReader | FileWriter vs BufferedWriter

These pairs are closely related —
👉 the FileReader / FileWriter classes perform basic file reading/writing,
👉 while BufferedReader / BufferedWriter add a layer of efficiency (buffering).

| Feature         | `FileReader`                                     | `BufferedReader`                                |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**     | Reads characters from a file (basic reading)     | Reads text efficiently using a **buffer**       |
| **Speed**       | Slower — reads one character at a time           | Faster — reads large chunks at once (buffered)  |
| **Buffering**   | ❌ No internal buffer                             | ✅ Uses an internal buffer (default 8KB)         |
| **Read Method** | `read()` (single char)                           | `readLine()` (whole line), `read()`             |
| **Usage**       | Good for small files or simple character reading | Best for reading large text files, line-by-line |


 Feature | `FileWriter` | `BufferedWriter` |
|----------|---------------|------------------|
| **Purpose** | Writes characters directly to a file | Writes characters efficiently using a **buffer** |
| **Speed** | Slower — writes each character directly to disk | Faster — stores in memory first, writes in bulk |
| **Buffering** | ❌ No buffer | ✅ Uses an internal buffer (default 8KB) |
| **Convenience** | Must handle writing manually | Has extra methods like `newLine()` |
| **Usage** | For simple or small writes | For large data or frequent writes |


| Pair    | Basic Class | Buffered Class | Difference                                      |
| ------- | ----------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Reading | FileReader  | BufferedReader | BufferedReader is faster, supports `readLine()` |
| Writing | FileWriter  | BufferedWriter | BufferedWriter is faster, supports `newLine()`  |

FileReader/FileWriter perform simple character-level file operations directly.
BufferedReader/BufferedWriter add a memory buffer, improving performance by reducing disk I/O and allowing convenient methods like readLine() and newLine()
